// =====================================================================
// D01 - UAC Bypass via Registry Hijack of Auto-Elevating Binaries
// =====================================================================
// MITRE ATT&CK : T1548.002 - Abuse Elevation Control Mechanism: Bypass UAC
// Tactic       : Privilege Escalation, Defense Evasion
// Severity     : High
// Data source  : Sysmon Event ID 13 (Registry value set)
// Author       : Umang Udeshi
//
// Description:
//   Attackers write a command into a shell\open\command key in the user's
//   own registry hive (HKCU\Software\Classes). When an auto-elevating
//   Windows binary (sdclt, fodhelper, computerdefaults, eventvwr) runs,
//   it reads that key and launches the attacker's command with admin
//   rights - without a UAC prompt.
//
// Tested against: OTRF APT29 Day 1 (196,081 events)
//   True positives : 2 (22:58:18 and 22:58:30 UTC on SCRANTON)
//   False positives: 0
//
// Known false positives:
//   Rare. Some software installers legitimately register file handlers.
//   Tune by excluding known installer processes in Image if needed.
//
// Essential Eight mitigation:
//   Restrict administrative privileges - standard users cannot gain
//   admin rights through this bypass. Setting UAC to "Always notify"
//   also blocks this technique family.
// =====================================================================
let hijackKeys = dynamic([
    "\\Folder\\shell\\open\\command",       // sdclt.exe
    "\\exefile\\shell\\open\\command",      // sdclt.exe (older variant)
    "\\ms-settings\\shell\\open\\command",  // fodhelper.exe, computerdefaults.exe
    "\\mscfile\\shell\\open\\command"       // eventvwr.exe
]);
Apt29Raw
| where Channel == "Microsoft-Windows-Sysmon/Operational" and EventID == 13
| where TargetObject has "_Classes" and TargetObject has_any (hijackKeys)
| project EventTime, Hostname, Image, TargetObject, Details
| order by EventTime asc
